## Overview of ASET/RSET Methodology
The design approach outlined in this section is commonly referred to as ASET - RSET and has been used here to justify the extended travel distances provided within the warehouse. The ASET - RSET methodology consists of a comparison between calculated escape times (Available Safe Egress Time - ASET) and the time taken for untenable conditions to occur (Required Safe Egress Time - RSET). This methodology is illustrated in Figure {fig:aset_rset{{ k }}}.
!figblock aset_rset{{ k }} | aset_rset_diagram

## Mathematical Correlations and Associated Definitions
= Design Requirement = TASET > TRSET
= Margin of Safety = TMARGIN = TASET – TRSET
= TRSET = TDET + TALARM + TPRE + TTRAV
= TASET – Available Safe Escape Time (ASET)
The calculated time available between ignition of a fire and the time at which tenability criteria are exceeded in a specified space in a building.
= TRSET – Required Safe Escape Time (RSET)
Calculated time available between ignition of a fire and the time at which occupants in a specified space in a building are able to reach a place of safety.
= TDET – Time to Detection
The time from ignition to detection either by an automatic system or by an occupant seeing the fire and raising an alarm.
= TALARM – Time to Alarm
The time from detection to a general alarm being raised within the building.
= TPRE – Pre-movement Time
Consists of two distinct behavioural elements for each occupant: the recognition time (the time from alarm until the occupant begins to respond) and the response time (the time taken by the occupant to undertake activities – fire investigation, fire-fighting, halting machine operation, collecting members of a group and / or belongings – prior to commencing the travel phase of the evacuation).
= TTRAV – Travel Time
The time required to evacuate to a place of relative safety. This consists of two components: the time required to walk to an exit; the time required to flow through the elements (doors, corridors, stairs) forming the escape route.

## Design Requirement
The available safe escape time must be greater than the required safe escape time.

## Calculation of ASET
The ASET has been calculated using the methodology provided in Drysdale[4] and BS 7974-2[5]. Full details of the calculations, and the numerical assumptions made, are provided in this section.
The calculations below are deployed at one second intervals to allow the temperature, depth and volume of the smoke layer to be measured over time. A {{ assessment_minutes }}-minute assessment period has been adopted, which is sufficient to account for the time taken to evacuate the space.{% if not aset_none %} The calculations are terminated 30 seconds after the smoke layer breaches 2m height.{% endif %}
Unless otherwise specified, the same parameters are used for all {{ number_of_buildings }} of the units.
{% if shared_params_differ %}
!! The units were run with different values for {{ shared_params_differ }}. The shared parameters quoted below are those of {{ buildings[0].name }}; state the others here or align the inputs.
{% endif %}
### Heat Release Rate of the Fire
An uncapped growing fire has been assumed. For a given time, the heat release rate can be calculated as per equation 22 of BS 7974-1[6], as follows:
[[eq_hrr]]
> where:
> [[Q]] = Heat release rate at the given time (kW);
> [[t]] = Time from ignition (s);
> [[alpha]] = Fire growth rate coefficient (kW/s²).
{% if growth_rate_is_ultra_fast %}
The fire growth rate coefficient should be selected based on the contents of the space and their likely fire growth rate. As the contents of the warehouses are, at this stage, unknown, a worst-case assumption has been made that the contents will exhibit an “Ultra-Fast” growth rate. Therefore, the fire growth rate coefficient is 0.188 kW/s². This is a conservative assumption because if the contents of the warehouses were to exhibit an ultra-fast growth rate, sprinkler protection would need to be provided which would reduce fire growth significantly once activated (6.5, BS 9999[1]).
{% else %}
The fire growth rate coefficient should be selected based on the contents of the space and their likely fire growth rate. A {{ growth_rate_label }} growth rate has been assumed for the contents of the warehouses. Therefore, the fire growth rate coefficient is {{ fgr }} kW/s².
!! The growth rate is not the default ultra-fast value. Justify the choice of {{ growth_rate_label }} growth here.
{% endif %}
The assumed fire curve for the model is shown in Figure {fig:hrr{{ k }}}. This figure shows both, the total heat release rate, and the convective heat release rate (taken to be 70% of the total) across the whole of the {{ assessment_minutes }}-minute reference period.
!fig hrr{{ k }} | Heat Release Rate Over the {{ assessment_minutes }}-minute Reference Period
