## Calculation of RSET
This section outlines the assumptions and calculation method used to determine the RSET (i.e. the time from ignition to final occupant escape).
### Time to Detection
It is reasonable to expect that once fitted out, the warehouses will be fully covered by an alarm and detection system which includes detection within the warehouse. In the absence of other data, and to account for a range of possible detection systems, a time to detection of {{ detection_time }} seconds is conservatively assumed. This is considered to be a conservative assumption given the {{ growth_rate_label | lower }} growth rate of the fire, which would produce a large amount of smoke and would likely activate the detection system long before this time.
!! Check the detection assumptions against any proposed detection system for the buildings.
### Time to Alarm
It is reasonable to expect that once fitted out, the warehouses will be fully covered by an alarm and detection system which includes detection within the warehouse. On this basis, and assuming that the warehouse does not operate an investigation period, a time for the alarm to activate of 0 seconds has been assumed. This is in accordance with the guidance in PD 7974-6[8].
### Pre-movement Time
{% if pre_movement_is_default %}
A pre-movement time of 180 seconds has been assumed. This figure has been taken from guidance given in Table E.2 of PD 7974-6[8] and is the 99th percentile value for a space where the occupants are familiar with the layout of the building and “standard” management procedures are in place.
{% else %}
A pre-movement time of {{ pre_movement_time }} seconds has been assumed.
!! The pre-movement time is not the 180 s default from Table E.2 of PD 7974-6. Justify the value used.
{% endif %}
### Travel Time
As per CIBSE Guide E[7], {{ walking_speed }} m/s can be taken as an average speed for persons walking on flat surfaces. The maximum direct travel distances{% if fitout_known %} (with the indicative fit-out layouts){% endif %} and associated travel times are in Table {tab:travel_time{{ k }}}.
!table travel_time{{ k }} | Travel Time based on Maximum Travel Distances for Each Unit
### Queueing Time
{% if all_over_220 %}
The exit widths and maximum capacities have been sized using the guidance in BS 9999[1]. As noted in BS 7974-6 (Table G.4[8]), the maximum design sustained flow capacity implicit in the Approved Document B[3] guidance for a population in excess of 220 persons is 1.33 persons/m/s. This applies to all of the units. The estimated population, number of exit doors (one of which is discounted), widths and total travel time through the doors are given in Table {tab:doors{{ k }}}. Exit routes shared with the offices and secondary staircases are discounted.
{% else %}
The exit widths and maximum capacities have been sized using the guidance in BS 9999[1]. As noted in BS 7974-6 (Table G.4[8]), the maximum design sustained flow capacity implicit in the Approved Document B[3] guidance for a population in excess of 220 persons is 1.33 persons/m/s. This applies to {{ over_220_names }}. For the remaining units, with lower occupancy, and door widths greater than the minimum of 1050mm, the maximum flow capacity is given as 1.4 persons/m/s. As a conservative choice, the lower rate of 1.33 persons/m/s has been used for all units in the calculations for the travel time through the exit doors. The estimated population, number of exit doors (one of which is discounted), widths and total travel time through the doors are given in Table {tab:doors{{ k }}}. Exit routes shared with the offices and secondary staircases are discounted.
{% endif %}
